%[text] ## Overview of Optical Flow Theory
%[text] In [EdgeDetectionApplied.m](file:../../Scripts/EdgeDetectionApplied.m) and [RegionSegmentation.m](file:../../Scripts/RegionSegmentation.m), we focused on individual images. As we transition from analyzing single images to sequences of frames or videos, we can begin to understand dynamic scenes more comprehensively. Optical flow is essential in this process, helping us enhance video quality, segment objects, track their movement, and even recognize complex events over time.
%[text] 
%[text] **Optical flow** is a technique used to estimate the motion of objects as perceived in a sequence of images. It calculates the movement of brightness patterns, corresponding to apparent motion. Apparent motion refers to the perception of movement in a visual scene, even when there is no actual movement, such as in optical illusions or animations. In contrast, actual motion involves the physical displacement of objects in space, like a plane flying in the sky. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Two side-by-side images of a plane in flight. The left image shows optical flow vectors as arrows, while the right image shows the plane without overlays.](text:image:4a55)
%[text] **Optical flow** estimates apparent motion by analyzing changes in pixel intensity in image sequences, which can approximate actual motion under certain conditions. The main goal is to determine the motion of each pixel by tracking the movement of brightness patterns across consecutive frames. This is achieved by calculating **optical flow vectors**, which describe the direction and speed of motion for every point in the scene. These vectors effectively map the apparent movement within the video, providing valuable insights into how objects, surfaces, and edges shift over time.
%[text] Optical flow estimates the movement of each pixel between two time points by tracking brightness patterns from one image to the next. We can estimate the motion or the brightness between one frame at $I(x,y,t) ${"altText":"Equation representing the intensity II at coordinates x and y at time t."} and the subsequent frame at $I(x,y,t+\\delta{t})${"altText":"Equation representing the intensity II at coordinates x and y at time t plus a small time increment delta t."}. So we are following pixels from one time point to a second time point at $t= t+\\delta{t}${"altText":"The equation representing time t plus a small time increment delta t."}.
%[text] ![](text:image:25b6) **Exercise 1.** In the context of optical flow, what do optical flow vectors describe?
Ans1 = "The direction and speed of motion of features within an image."; % Non-default options include: "The change in brightness patterns over time.", "The direction and speed of motion of features within an image.", "The static position of pixels in an image." and "The random movement of pixels." %[control:dropdown:0d84]{"position":[8,72]}
CheckAnswer("Exercise1",Ans1)
%%
%[text] To calculate optical flow, we rely on two key assumptions:
%[text]{"align":"center"} **Assumption 1**. *Brightness Constancy.* Assume that the brightness is the same for the pixels representing each object over time. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} **Assumption 2**. *Small Motion*. Assume that the motion of an object from one frame to the next is small.                                          
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_970e] ### Assumption 1. Brightness Constancy
%[text] As you vary $t${"altText":"The variable t represents time."}, as a pixel moves, its brightness remains the same. This is given by:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $I(x(t),y(t),t)= \\text{Constant}${"altText":"Equation representing the intensity I as a function of x(t), y(t), and t, where the result is a constant value."}
%[text] Consider a time sequence t ranging from $t = 1${"altText":"t = 1"} to $t = n${"altText":"t = n"}. The corresponding image sequence is represented by the frame index $k${"altText":"k"}, where $k${"altText":"k"} spans from $t${"altText":"t"} to $t+\\delta{t}${"altText":"t+\delta{t}"}. For $ (x(1),y(1))${"altText":"The coordinates (x(1),y(1)) represent a specific point in space."} until $ (x(k),y(k))${"altText":"The coordinates (x(k),y(k)) represent a specific point in space at scene k."}, the intensity (or brightness) of this point is the same for all frames. 
%[text] ![](text:image:9dfa) **Try**. Visualize [Assumption 1](internal:M_970e) to track five points, each corresponding to a specific pixel, across different frames and calculate their intensity in each frame. Optical flow vectors describing the apparent motion of each of the points between two frames are shown on the first frame. Use the colorbar to determine if the intensity for each pixel remains constant. You will observe that each point, maintains a consistent intensity regardless of the frame number.
numFramesA = 3; %[control:slider:04d4]{"position":[14,15]}
[~] = GenScatPlot(numFramesA,false,true);
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_083a] ### Assumption 2. Small Motion
%[text] We begin by tracking one pixel $(x,y)${"altText":"The pixel coordinates are given by x comma y."}, represented by a colored-dot, that is traveling at a velocity of $(u,v)${"altText":"The coordinate pair u and v represent the velocity or optical flow in the x and y directions. "} from frame $I(x,y,t) ${"altText":"Equation representing the intensity II at coordinates x and y at time t."} to a consecutive frame $I(x,y,t+\\delta{t})${"altText":"Equation representing the intensity II at coordinates x and y at time t plus a small time increment delta t."}. The pixel's new coordinates are $(x + u\\delta{t}, y+v\\delta{t})${"altText":"The coordinates of x plus u times delta t, and y plus v times delta t. "}, where the displacements are given by: 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ($\\delta{x},\\delta{y})=(u\\delta{t},v\\delta{t})${"altText":"\delta{x},\delta{y})=(u\delta{t},v\delta{t})"}.
%[text] ![](text:image:5536) **Try**. Visualize the motion of a pixel traveling from one frame to a consecutive frame.
  %[control:button:87a7]{"position":[1,2]}
x1 = 10+30*rand(1,1); % Generate a random x coordinate between 10 and 40
y1 = 10+30*rand(1,1); % Generate a random y coordinate between 10 and 40

figure("Units","Normalized",OuterPosition=[0 0 0.5 0.5]) % Set figure to fullscreen
tiledlayout(1,2)
nexttile
scatter(x1,y1,"filled",SeriesIndex=1)
text(x1,y1,"($x,y$)",Interpreter="latex",FontSize=14,Position=[x1-0.5 y1-0.5],VerticalAlignment="top",HorizontalAlignment="right");
delta = 5; % Define a small delta for movement
x2 = x1+delta;
y2 = y1+delta;

hold on
scatter(x2,y2)
quiver(x1,y1,(x2-x1),(y2-y1),1,LineWidth=1.5,MaxHeadSize=1,AutoScale="on"); % Include quiver vectors for the original points
hold off

text(x2,y2,"($x+u\delta{t},y+u\delta{t}$)",Interpreter="latex",FontSize=14,Position=[x2+5 y2+5],VerticalAlignment="bottom",HorizontalAlignment="center");

% Set axis properties
title("$I(x,y,t)$",FontSize=14,Interpreter="latex")
xticks([])
yticks([])
axis padded
ax = gca;
ax.Box = "on";
ax.XLim = [0 70];
ax.YLim = [0 70];

nexttile
scatter(x2,y2,"filled")
text(x2,y2,"($x+u\delta{t},y+u\delta{t}$)",Interpreter="latex",FontSize=14,Position=[x2+5 y2+5],VerticalAlignment="bottom",HorizontalAlignment="center");

% Set axis properties
title("$I(x+\delta{x},y+\delta{y},t+\delta{t})$",FontSize=14,Interpreter="latex")
xticks([])
yticks([])
axis padded
ax = gca;
ax.Box = "on";
ax.XLim = [0 70];
ax.YLim = [0 70];
%[text] If we assume that the distance a pixel travels in time $\\delta{t}${"altText":"\delta{t}"} is small, then $ t + \\delta{t}${"altText":"The variable t plus delta t."} is equivalently $t${"altText":"t"}. Therefore for a really small space-time step, the brightness of the image point remains constant over time as shown in this equation: 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\text {Brightness of a Pixel: } I(x+u\\delta{t},y+v\\delta{t},t+\\delta{t}) = I(x,y,t)${"altText":"The equation shows the intensity II at a point (x,y) at time t equals the intensity at a new point x plus u times delta t, y plus u times delta at a later time, t + delta t."}
%%
%[text] ### Brightness Constancy Equation
%[text] With the two assumptions, for a small space and small time step, we have an equivalent brightness between two consecutive frames. This gives us the Brightness Constancy Equation:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{\\partial I}{\\partial x} \\frac{dx}{dt} + \\frac{\\partial I}{\\partial y}  \\frac{dy}{dt} + \\frac{\\partial I}{\\partial t} = 0${"altText":"The total derivative of intensity I with respect to time t equals the sum of the partial derivative of I with respect to x times the derivative of x with respect to t, the partial derivative of I with respect to y times the derivative of y with respect to t, and the partial derivative of I with respect to t."} $(\\star)${"altText":"(\star)"}
%[text] which simplifies to:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $&dollar&;I\_xu+I\_yv+I\_t=0&dollar&;${"altText":"The equation consists of three terms involving partial derivatives of a function I with respect to x, y, and t. The partial derivatives with respect to x and y are multiplied by u and v, respectively, and the sum of these three components equals zero."}
%[text] where $I\_x${"altText":"I\_x"} and $I\_y${"altText":"I\_y"} represent the spatial derivative of image brightness, $I\_t${"altText":"I\_t"} represents the temporal derivative, and $u${"altText":"u"} and $v${"altText":"v"} represent the velocities, or the optical flow vectors in the x and y direction. We aim to determine the optical flow vectors $u${"altText":"u"} and $v${"altText":"v"} for each point in the image. In this equation, the unknowns $u${"altText":"u"} and $v${"altText":"v"} represent the optical flow vectors. This means we cannot uniquely determine $u${"altText":"u"} and $v${"altText":"v"} for every point, resulting in an unconstrained system. By expressing this equation in vector form, it becomes evident that we have one equation with two unknowns, $u${"altText":"u"} and $v${"altText":"v"}.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\nabla{I}\\cdot\[u,v\]^T+I\_t=0${"altText":"\nabla{I}\cdot\[u,v\]^T+I\_t=0"}
%[text] There are a few different approaches to providing the additional constraint to make the Brightness Constancy equation $(\\star)${"altText":"(\star)"} solvable including the Lucas-Kanade method, Horn-Schunck Method, and the Lucas-Kanade Derivative of Gaussian Method.
%[text] ![](text:image:3183) **Exercise 2.** What is the primary challenge in solving the Brightness Constancy Equation?
Ans2 = "The equation has too many unknowns and not enough constraints."; % Non-default options include: "The equation has too many unknowns and not enough constraints.", "The equation is non-linear and difficult to solve.", "The equation does not account for changes in brightness." and "The equation is only applicable to static images." %[control:dropdown:8896]{"position":[8,72]}
CheckAnswer("Exercise2",Ans2)
%%
%[text] ## Lucas-Kanade(LK) Method
%[text] ### Assumption 3. Spatial Coherence
%[text] If instead of looking at individual pixels, we look at a small patch of pixels and assume they share a single velocity, we assume spatial coherence. Mathematically, now, instead of having only one equation with two unknowns, we now have multiple equations for the pixels within the region.  Each pixel in the region contributes to the brightness constancy equation. If we have $5x5${"altText":"5x5"} image patch, for each pixel, $p${"altText":"p"}, we have 25 equations.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ${\\begin{array}{c} I\_x(p\_1)u+y(p\_1)v=I\_t(p\_1) \\\\I\_x(p\_2)u+I+y(p\_2)v=I\_t(p\_2) \\\\ \\vdots \\\\ I\_x(p\_{25})u+I+y(p\_{25})v=I\_t(p\_{25}) \\end{array}}\n${"altText":"A system of 25 linear equations for optical flow, where each equation combines image gradients in x and y directions with the temporal gradient."}
%[text] If we look at this equation in matrix form, we get: $A${"altText":"A"}is a $25\\times 2${"altText":"25\times 2"} matrix, $x${"altText":"x"} is a $2\\times 1${"altText":"2\times 1"}vector, and $b${"altText":"b"} is a $25\\times 1${"altText":"25\times 1"}vector.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\underbrace{\\left \[\\matrix{I\_x(p\_1) & I\_y(p\_1) \\cr I\_x(p\_2)&  I\_y(p\_2)  \\cr \\vdots &\\vdots\\cr  I\_x(p\_{25})&  I\_y(p\_{25})} \\right \]}\_\\text{A(25x2)}\n\\underbrace{\\left\[\\matrix{u \\cr v}\\right\]}\_\\text{x(2x1)} = -\n\\underbrace{\\left\[\\begin{array}{c} I\_t(p\_1) \\\\ I\_t(p\_2) \\\\ \\vdots \\\\ I\_t(p\_{25}) \\end{array} \\right\]}\_\\text{b(25x1)}\n${"altText":"An optical flow matrix equation with image gradients and velocities on the left, and temporal gradients on the right."}
%[text]  By assuming the same motion vector for all pixels in the patch, we create an overconstrained system with 25 equations and the same two unknowns, $u${"altText":"u"} and $v${"altText":"v"}.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ${\\bf{A}}x=b${"altText":"Matrix A multiplied by x equals b."} $(\\star\\star)${"altText":"(\star\star)"}
%[text]  This system, $\\star\\star${"altText":"\star\star"}, is then solved using a least-squares approximation, which finds the best-fit solution for the motion vector by minimizing the sum of squared intensity differences across the neighborhood. The least-squares approximation, tells us:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\hat{x} = \\underset{x}{\\text{arg min}} \\, ||\\mathbf{A}x - b||^2${"altText":"\hat{x} = \underset{x}{\text{arg min}} , ||\mathbf{A}x - b||^2"} is equivalent to solving ${\\bf{A}}^T{\\bf{A}}\\hat{x}={\\bf{A}}^Tb${"altText":"A transpose times A, multiplied by x hat, equals A transpose times b"}.
%[text] To obtain the least-squares approximation, we solve:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\left \[\\matrix{\\sum\_{p \\in P} I\_x I\_x & \\sum\_{p \\in P} I\_x I\_y \\cr\\sum\_{p \\in P} I\_y I\_x& \\sum\_{p \\in P} I\_y I\_y  \\cr } \\right \]\n\\left\[\\matrix{u \\cr v}\\right\] = -\n\\left \[\\matrix{\\sum\_{p \\in P} I\_x I\_t \\cr \\sum\_{p \\in P} I\_y I\_t } \\right \]\n${"altText":"A 2x2 matrix of image gradient summations in x and y directions, multiplied by a 2x1 optical flow vector (\[u, v\]), equals the negative of a 2x1 matrix of image gradient summations in x and t directions."}
%[text] where the summation is over each pixel p in patch $\\bf{P}${"altText":"P \n"}. This approximation allows us to solve for our two unknowns, $u${"altText":"u"} and $v${"altText":"v"}, our optical flow vectors.
%[text] Given that the Lucas-Kanade (LK) method assumes that the optical flow is constant within a small window around each pixel, it performs well in areas with high texture or corners and is effective when the displacement between frames is small. This method is ideal for **object tracking** and **motion detection** in scenarios where the motion is relatively minor. However, the LK method has a notable limitation: it is highly sensitive to noise.
%[text] ![](text:image:1a1d) **Demonstration**. Visualize the small patch, $&dollar&;\\bf{P}&dollar&;${"altText":"\\$\bf{P}\\$"}, moving at one velocity.
  %[control:button:74b5]{"position":[1,2]}
VizSpacCoherence;
%%
%[text] If we zoom in on the small patch, $\\bf{P}${"altText":"P \n"}, defined by a blue square with 5 pixels from the demonstration above, we can see that the pixels moving in the same direction inside the patch, all with the same optical flow vector. In this way, we satisfy spatial coherence to solve for $u${"altText":"u"} and $v${"altText":"v"}. 
%[text] ![](text:image:9dde) **Try**. Observe the pixels in the small patch moving at the same velocity.
numFramesB =3; %[control:slider:5439]{"position":[13,14]}
intensityTableA = GenScatPlot(numFramesB,true,false);
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:5620) **Exercise 3.** What is the primary advantage of using the Lucas-Kanade method for optical flow estimation?
Ans3 = "It performs well with minimal motion."; % Non-default options include: "It performs well with minimal motion." and "It ignores the brightness constancy equation." %[control:dropdown:81ad]{"position":[8,47]}
CheckAnswer("Exercise3",Ans3)
%%
%[text] ## Lucas-Kanade Derivative of Gaussian (LK-DoG) Method
%[text] What if the small motion assumption does not hold? Imagine, you are taking a video with your phone, and you are not using using a tripod. Now, your camera is moving and your video is unstable and so now we have a case where [Assumption 2 ](internal:M_083a)no longer holds. 
%[text] In the previous section, you decreased the image resolution to force the validity of [Assumption 1](internal:M_970e). In the LK-DoG method, images are first blurred with a Gaussian filter to reduce noise and improve gradient calculations. Spatial and temporal image gradients are then computed to formulate optical flow equations. These equations, based on the assumption that pixel intensity remains constant between frames, [Assumption 1](internal:M_970e), are solved using the least squares criterion. This process estimates the optical flow vector for each pixel, indicating motion direction and magnitude, and provides a detailed motion map across the scene.
%[text] If we take an image and reduce its size, we get a less-detailed or blurrier version of the original. By continuing to decrease the size, the image becomes increasingly less detailed until it is so blurred that large differences in motion are no longer noticeable. This process of creating multiple levels of increasingly smaller and less detailed images is called a resolution pyramid or a coarse to fine estimation.
%[text] By using a resolution pyramid, we can ensure that certain variables remain small. Eventually, all resolutions will be reduced to less than a pixel in size, validating the small motion assumption once more. This reduction in resolution also results in image blurring.
%[text] The Lucas-Kanade Derivative of Gaussian (LK-DoG) method builds on the original Lucas-Kanade method, offering improved noise handling. This method is ideal for scenarios where noise reduction and smoothing are crucial, such as **tracking objects** in noisy video sequences. However, the LK-DoG method has a significant limitation: it is computationally intensive.
%[text] ![](text:image:6dca) **Try**. Adjust the image resolution and blur the pixels to compute lower resolution images. Try to adjust the resolution so that the line going down the image covers approximately the same pixel ranges for the $t${"altText":"t"} and $t+\\delta{t}${"altText":"t+\delta{t}"} images.
%[text] 1. *(Optional) Press button to preview image frames.*
%[text] 2. *Move slider to adjust image resolution to blur the image.* \
  %[control:button:8f83]{"position":[1,2]}
n = 1; % Starting frame number
ChosenVid = "FlowerYellow.mp4";

FlowerVid = VideoReader(ChosenVid); % Create a video reader object
frameNumb = FlowerVid.NumFrames;
firstFrame = read(FlowerVid,n);
secondFrame = read(FlowerVid,n+5); % Read the frame that displays a large motion difference

% Clear the VideoReader object to free up resources
clear FlowerVid;

T = im2double(firstFrame); % Convert the first frame to double for processing
TplusdelT = im2double(secondFrame); % Convert the second frame to double for processing

figure
tiledlayout(2,2,TileSpacing="none",Padding="compact") % Set up tiled layout
%
nexttile(1) % Tile (1,1)
imshow(T) % t at resolution original
ylabel("$t$",Interpreter="latex",HorizontalAlignment="left",FontSize=12,Rotation=0) % Insert y-axis label
ax = gca;
ax.XAxisLocation = "top";
xlabel("Resolution of $1$",Interpreter="latex",FontSize=12)

nexttile (3) % Tile (2,1)
imshow(TplusdelT) % t + delta t at resolution original
ylabel("$t+\delta{t}$",Interpreter="latex",HorizontalAlignment="center",FontSize=12,Rotation=0)
%%
%[text] Try to adjust the resolution so that the blue line drawn on the image covers approximately the same pixel ranges for the $t${"altText":"t"} and $t+\\delta{t}${"altText":"t+\delta{t}"} images.
Na = 5; %[control:slider:7877]{"position":[6,7]}
if exist("firstFrame","var") && exist("secondFrame","var")
    nexttile(2) % Tile (1,2)
    ResPyr(Na,firstFrame,1)
    ax = gca;
    ax.XAxisLocation = "top";
    NaNew=num2str(Na);
    xlabeltext = "Resolution of $"+NaNew+"$";
    xlabel(xlabeltext,Interpreter="latex",FontSize=12);

    nexttile(4) % Tile (2,2)
    ResPyr(Na,secondFrame,1)
else
    warning("Preview image frames above.")
end
