%[text] ## Viewing Optical Flow Results
%[text] There are a variety of ways to visualize optical flow results, each serving different purposes and use cases:
%[text] 1. **Frame Sequence and Vectors**: Viewing the initial frame, the subsequent frame, and then plotting the optical flow vectors helps understand the progression of motion between frames. This method is ideal for analyzing the direction and magnitude of motion over time, such as in video editing or scientific research.
%[text] 2. **Overlay Vectors on Frames**: Viewing the initial frame with optical flow vectors overlaid, followed by the second frame, allows you to see how motion vectors relate to specific objects within the frames. This approach is useful in applications like object tracking in video surveillance or sports analysis.
%[text] 3. **Motion Color Intensity Map**: Overlaying the optical flow vectors on the first frame and then plotting the corresponding motion flow vector image heatmap visually interprets the magnitude of motion using color coding. This method is particularly valuable in scenarios like autonomous driving or environmental monitoring, where understanding motion patterns and their intensity is essential for decision-making. \
%[text] ![](text:image:8c99) **Try**.  Visualize these optical flow views by looking at each of these views of a turkey walking through traffic
%[text] 1. *Select a display mode.*
%[text] 2. *Select a starting point frame number.*
%[text] 3. *Press 'View Results of the Optical Flow' to see the outcome.* \
VidDisp = "Turkey.mp4";
DisPlayA = "Frame sequence and vectors"; % Non-default options include: "Frame sequence and vectors", "Overlay vector on frame" and "Motion color intensity map" %[control:dropdown:665c]{"position":[12,40]}
FrameNum = 2; %[control:spinner:0f37]{"position":[12,13]}
  %[control:button:8321]{"position":[1,2]}
[~,numFramesV]= OptFlow(VidDisp,opticalFlowLK,FrameNum,DisPlayA);
%%
%[text] ### Lucas-Kanade Method Applied
%[text] ![](text:image:821c) **Try.** Use the Lucas-Kanade method to track the motion of a flower.
%[text] 1. *Select a display mode.*
%[text] 2. *Select a starting point frame number.*
%[text] 3. *Press 'View Results of the Lucas-Kanade Method' to see the outcome.* \
DisPlayLK = "Overlay vector on frame"; % Non-default values include: "Frame sequence and vectors", "Overlay vector on frame", and "Motion color intensity map" %[control:dropdown:3046]{"position":[13,38]}
VidLK = "FlowerPurple.mp4";
opticflowLK = opticalFlowLK;
if ~strcmp(VidLK,"Select")
    FrameNum = 2; %[control:spinner:1605]{"position":[16,17]}
      %[control:button:9d6c]{"position":[5,6]}
    [~,~]= OptFlow(VidLK,opticalFlowLK,FrameNum,DisPlayLK);
else
    warning("Select a valid option.") %#ok<*UNRCH>
end
